+<script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"Are Myrtle Beach condo prices dropping?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes, slowly. The typical metro condo is worth about 11.5 percent less than at its October 2023 high, and about 1 percent less than a year ago. House values held."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why are HOA fees so high in Myrtle Beach?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Oceanfront buildings charge the most: the middle oceanfront condo for sale charges about $925 a month, against about $650 for all condos. The fee usually includes the building's insurance."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Are Myrtle Beach condos in a flood zone?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"About 1 in 6 condos sold in Horry County in the past year sat in a high-risk flood zone. About 1 in 10 other homes did. We read the building's flood zone and master policy for you."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Do you need to live in a Myrtle Beach condo to get the lower tax rate?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Yes. The 4 percent rate is for the home its owner lives in, once the owner applies for it. A rental or a second home is taxed on 6 percent of its value."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is North Myrtle Beach or Myrtle Beach cheaper for a condo?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"The City of Myrtle Beach. Condos there sold for a middle price of about $175,000, against about $340,000 in North Myrtle Beach."}}]}</script>
