high again until October 2021, about 15 years later. The house did in August 2020, about 13 years later.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In September 2026 the typical condo was worth about $231,000, about 11.5 percent below its October 2023 high. The typical house, about $364,000, was under 2 percent below its own high.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto;margin:1.2rem 0;max-width:760px"><table aria-label="Typical condo and house value in the Myrtle Beach metro" style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:.92rem"><thead><tr><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">When</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Typical condo</th><th style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);color:var(--navy);text-align:left;font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.8rem;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase">Typical house</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Late 2001</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$112,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$146,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">High, 2006 or 2007</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$197,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$243,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Low, 2011 or 2012</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$113,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$169,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Latest month</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$231,000</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">$364,000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Since 2001</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Up 107%</td><td style="padding:.55rem .8rem;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);color:var(--muted)">Up 149%</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The typical metro condo is now worth about 64 percent of the typical house. In mid-2006 it was about 83 percent, the highest in 25 years. Only 2 other months since late 2001 were lower: May and June 2021.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">For an investor, a condo here is cheap next to a house today. Its value record shows no steady growth, so buy it for its income and its lower price.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="prices-to-buy"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Prices to buy">What do condos cost to buy in Myrtle Beach and North Myrtle Beach?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About $224,000 was the middle price of about <span style="white-space:nowrap">2,600 condos</span> sold in Horry County in the past year. Half sold between about $155,000 and $325,000.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Middle condo sale price in Horry County, by area"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Middle condo sale price in Horry County, by area</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Area, sales</span><span role="columnheader">middle price</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">North Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">694 condo sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$340,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Outside city limits and other towns</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">1,170 condo sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.588" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$200,000</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">City of Myrtle Beach</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">704 condo sales</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.514" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$175,000</span></span></div><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every other kind of home</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">10,600 sales, for comparison</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.959" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">$326,000</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Deeds Horry County recorded in the past year. The areas follow city limits. Every other kind of home means houses, townhouses outside a condo and mobile homes on owned land.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">North Myrtle Beach condos sell for about twice the City of Myrtle Beach's. Asking prices in oceanfront buildings show the same split.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">How cheap can a Myrtle Beach condo be?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 4 in 10 condos sold for under $200,000, against about 1 in 7 other homes. Today about 1,200 condos for sale ask under $200,000, and about 170 ask under $100,000.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Oceanfront units still start under $200,000: about half of the 1,070 condos for sale in oceanfront buildings ask less. Most oceanfront units for sale are small, 629 of them studios or one-bedrooms.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="yearly-cost"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Yearly cost">How much does it cost to own a Myrtle Beach condo each year?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About $18,100 a year before insurance and the loan, for the middle two-bedroom for sale in an oceanfront building with a Myrtle Beach address. The HOA fee is most of it.</p><figure class="c3-cd-fig2"><div class="c3-cd-bill" role="table" aria-label="What one typical oceanfront condo costs to own for a year, before insurance and the loan"><p class="hd" aria-hidden="true">One typical oceanfront condo, a year</p><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">HOA fee <small>$1,075 a month</small></span><span role="cell">$12,900</span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader">Property tax at the rental rate <small>City of Myrtle Beach, before the 25 percent exemption</small></span><span role="cell">$5,200</span></div><div role="row" class="tot"><span role="rowheader">Total before insurance and the loan</span><span role="cell">$18,100</span></div><p class="nt">The middle two-bedroom for sale in an oceanfront building with a Myrtle Beach address asks about $340,000. Not in the total: insurance on the unit, the loan, utilities the fee does not cover, management and cleaning.</p></div></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Outside city limits, at the base unincorporated rate, the tax on the same condo would be about $4,100; some fire districts add more.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why are HOA fees so high in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The middle condo for sale charges a homeowners association (HOA) fee of about $650 a month. In oceanfront buildings the middle fee is about $925. About 1 in 5 condos for sale charge more than $1,000 a month.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The fee usually includes the building's insurance, which a house owner pays alone. An older building may also charge a special assessment for a large repair. We read the reserve study and look for one voted or planned.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What property tax does a rental condo pay?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A home that is not its owner's legal residence, such as a unit rented out year-round or a second home, is taxed on 6 percent of its value.</p><style>.c3-cc{margin:1.4rem 0 1.8rem;max-width:960px}.c3-cc table{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:var(--read,1.0625rem);line-height:1.45;color:var(--muted)}.c3-cc caption{caption-side:top;text-align:left;color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;line-height:1.4;padding:0 0 .7rem}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{padding:.6rem .7rem;text-align:left;vertical-align:top;border-bottom:1px solid var(--rule);overflow-wrap:break-word}.c3-cc thead th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;font-size:1em;border-bottom:2px solid var(--navy);vertical-align:bottom}.c3-cc tbody th{color:var(--navy);font-weight:600}.c3-cc .n{text-align:right;font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums}.c3-cc .i{display:inline-flex;vertical-align:-4px;margin-right:.45rem;color:var(--brass-ink)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:#fff}@media (max-width:39.999em){.c3-cc table,.c3-cc tbody,.c3-cc tr,.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{display:block;width:auto}.c3-cc caption{display:block}.c3-cc thead{position:absolute;width:1px;height:1px;overflow:hidden;clip:rect(0 0 0 0);white-space:nowrap}.c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory-2);border-top:3px solid var(--brass);margin:0 0 .8rem;padding:.5rem 0 .6rem}section[style*="background:var(--ivory-2)"] .c3-cc tbody tr{background:var(--ivory)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self{background:#fff!important;border:2px solid var(--brass)}.c3-cc tr.c3-self>*{background:transparent}.c3-cc th,.c3-cc td{border:0;padding:.2rem 1rem}.c3-cc tbody th{display:flex;align-items:center;font-size:1.1em;padding-bottom:.35rem}.c3-cc tbody th .i{flex:none;vertical-align:0}.c3-cc tbody th a{display:inline-block;padding:.5rem 0}.c3-cc .n{text-align:left}.c3-cc td[data-label]::before{content:attr(data-label) ": ";color:var(--navy);font-weight:500}}</style><div class="c3-cc"><table><caption>Property tax on a South Carolina condo, by how it is used</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">How the condo is used</th><th scope="col">Taxed on</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><th scope="row">The owner's legal residence</th><td data-label="Taxed on">4 percent of its value</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A unit rented out year-round</th><td data-label="Taxed on">6 percent of its value</td></tr><tr><th scope="row">A second home</th><td data-label="Taxed on">6 percent of its value</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A legal residence rented out more than 72 days in a calendar year loses the 4 percent rate, unless its owner is on active military duty under orders. A buyer of a unit already taxed at 6 percent may claim a 25 percent exemption. Our <a href="/invest/run-the-numbers/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental calculator</a> works out the tax for one unit.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Guests pay the lodging taxes on top of the price: about 13 percent on a stay under 90 days inside the City of Myrtle Beach. Our <a href="/invest/str-rules/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">short-term rental rules</a> page lists them.</p><div style="border-left:3px solid var(--brass);background:var(--ivory-2);padding:1.2rem 1.4rem;margin:1.8rem 0;max-width:720px"><p style="color:var(--navy);font-weight:600;margin-bottom:.4rem">Want the yearly cost for one building?</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;margin-bottom:.8rem">Send us the listing. We read the building's budget and rental rules and work out the fee and the tax for that unit before you offer.</p><a class="btn btn-brass" href="/contact/">Send us a building</a></div></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="renting-it"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Renting it">Can you rent out a Myrtle Beach condo by the night?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">In most buildings, yes. About 7 in 10 condos for sale in Horry County say they allow short-term rentals. About half list a vacation rental program.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Inside the City of Myrtle Beach, the city's zoning says where nightly rentals are legal. Our <a href="/submarkets/myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Myrtle Beach guide</a> has the city's map. We confirm both for any unit you want.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Who owns the condos here?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Few condos here are taxed as their owner's home. About 1 in 5 condo units is taxed at the 4 percent rate, which an owner claims for the home they live in. Of the county's other homes, about 6 in 10 are.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Homes in Horry County taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Homes in Horry County taxed at the 4 percent rate for a home its owner lives in</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Kind of home, how many</span><span role="columnheader">share</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Condo units</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 8,800 of 41,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.215" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 1 in 5</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every other kind of home</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 116,000 of 186,000</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.622" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 6 in 10</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>County parcel records. The rest are taxed at 6 percent: rentals, second homes and homes whose owner has not claimed the lower rate.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Most units in a condo building here are taxed at the rate for rentals and second homes. That is why the building's rules, its fee and its rental program matter so much.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What does a Myrtle Beach condo earn?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">We work out the rent one unit at a time. Our <a href="/invest/long-term-rental/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental analyzer</a> gives a first number for any address. One owner's real result:</p><aside class="c3-cd-story" aria-label="A real owner's numbers"><p class="lb">One owner's own numbers, not a promise</p><p>A friend of ours ran a hotel in Boston. When he retired, a house sale and his savings bought three condos in one oceanfront building here.</p><p>He had about $100,000 saved and sold his Massachusetts house for about $450,000. The three condos gross about $120,000 a year, and roughly half of it is profit. He now helps manage fourteen units.</p></aside><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Rental programs here are usually in oceanfront buildings run for short-term rentals. A buyer pays an HOA transfer fee and often a few months of dues into escrow. We compare the building's program with outside managers before you buy; our <a href="/invest/rental-program-vs-airbnb/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">rental program or Airbnb</a> page lists the fee lines.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="loans"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="Loans">Can you get a mortgage on a Myrtle Beach oceanfront condo?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Often not a normal one. Fannie Mae, one of the two companies that buy conventional loans, will not buy a loan on a unit in a building run like a hotel, even if each unit is owned separately. Signs it lists:</p><ul class="c3-cd-list c3-cd-cols"><li>registration services</li><li>the HOA or its manager renting units by the day or for short stays</li><li>daily cleaning</li><li>central key systems</li><li>a rental pool</li><li>rent shared with the HOA or a rental company</li></ul><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Fannie Mae also will not buy one in a building of 21 or more units where one person or company owns more than 20 percent of the units. The same goes for a building that needs critical repairs, for example repairs over $10,000 a unit due within 12 months that no special assessment pays for.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">What is a non-warrantable condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A building that fails these tests is called non-warrantable. Buildings with a rental program usually are, because so many owners are investors. That also shows the building is run for renting.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A non-warrantable building needs a larger down payment, so more of your cash stays in that unit and cannot fund the next purchase. A loan officer at our preferred lender can tell you which loans work, including a <a href="/invest/strategies/dscr-loans/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">DSCR loan</a> that is approved on the rent.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Can you use an FHA or VA loan on a Myrtle Beach condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">FHA loans, insured by the Federal Housing Administration, and VA loans for veterans are harder still. About 1 in 9 condo listings (307) say they accept FHA, and about 1 in 12 (228) VA.</p><figure class="c3-gchart"><div role="table" aria-label="Condos for sale in Horry County that say they take an FHA or VA loan"><span class="cap" aria-hidden="true">Condos for sale in Horry County that say they take an FHA or VA loan</span><div role="rowgroup" class="sr"><div role="row"><span role="columnheader">Listings, share</span><span role="columnheader">how many</span></div></div><div role="rowgroup"><div role="row" class="ref"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Every condo for sale</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">in Horry County</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:1" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">About 2,660</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Say they accept FHA</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 1 in 9</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.116" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">307</span></span></div><div role="row"><span role="rowheader"><span class="n">Say they accept VA</span><span class="dot" aria-hidden="true">&middot;</span><span class="f">about 1 in 12</span></span><span role="cell"><span class="b" style="--f:0.086" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="v">228</span></span></div></div></div><figcaption>Condos for sale on our site, counted in total.</figcaption></figure><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The federal housing department's FHA list holds only 9 approved condo projects in Horry County, so we confirm the building with the lender before you offer.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our guides to <a href="/invest/condotel-financing/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">condotel financing</a> and <a href="/invest/non-warrantable-condos/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">non-warrantable condos</a> go further.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory-2)" id="the-building"><div class="wrap"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3" data-toc="The building">What should you look for in a Myrtle Beach condo building?</h2><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The building's budget, reserves, rental rules and loan status matter more than the unit. Two units with the same view can cost and earn different amounts because their buildings differ.</p><div class="c3-aerial"><figure class="c3ph-hero" style=""><div class="c3ph-f"><img src="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp" srcset="/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-600w.webp 600w, /images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp 1200w" sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1136px" width="1200" height="675" alt="Two tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><span class="c3ph-cr"><a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" aria-label="Photo: USGS. License: Public domain" title="License: Public domain">Photo: USGS</a></span></div><figcaption>Condo towers and smaller condo buildings on the ocean in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane in 2011. Almost half of the county's 41,000 condo units are in complexes of 100 units or more.</figcaption></figure></div><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Before an offer, we read these:</p><ol class="c3-cd-steps"><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M15 9.4c-.4-1-1.5-1.8-3-1.8-1.8 0-3 1-3 2.2 0 3 6 1.6 6 4.4 0 1.3-1.3 2.2-3 2.2-1.6 0-2.7-.7-3.1-1.8M12 5.8v1.8M12 16.4v1.8"/><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/></svg></span><span><strong>The budget and the fee.</strong> What the fee pays for, how much it rose, and whether the association collects enough to pay its bills.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 5h16a1 1 0 0 1 1 1v14a1 1 0 0 1-1 1H4a1 1 0 0 1-1-1V6a1 1 0 0 1 1-1zM3 10h18M8 3v4M16 3v4M8 14h.01M12 14h.01M16 14h.01M8 17.5h.01M12 17.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>The reserve study and any assessment.</strong> How much is saved for roofs, elevators and balconies, and any <a href="/hoa/special-assessments/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">special assessment</a> voted or planned. Our <a href="/hoa/reserves/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">reserves page</a> explains a reserve study.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10.8 12.2 20 3M16.8 6.2l2.5 2.5M14.3 8.7l2 2"/><circle cx="7.5" cy="15.5" r="4.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>The rental rules and any rental program.</strong> Many communities ban renting at all. We filter those out and read the papers for any rule that would block your rental plan.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 11.5 12 3.5l9 8M5.5 10v10.5h13V10M10 20.5V15h4v5.5"/></svg></span><span><strong>The loan status.</strong> Whether a normal loan, an FHA loan or a VA loan works in the building. We confirm it with the lender.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 11.5 12 4.5l8 7M6.5 10v4.5M17.5 10v4.5M2 17c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0M2 20.5c1.7 1.2 3.3 1.2 5 0s3.3-1.2 5 0 3.3 1.2 5 0 3.3-1.2 5 0"/></svg></span><span><strong>The flood zone and the master policy.</strong> About 1 in 6 condos sold last year sat in a high-risk flood zone, against about 1 in 10 other homes. We read what the building's policy covers.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 21h18M5 21V9.5l5-3V21M10 21V4h9v17M13 8h3M13 12h3M13 16h3M7.5 13h.01M7.5 16.5h.01"/></svg></span><span><strong>Any lawsuit.</strong> Any case the association is part of. Our page on <a href="/buyers/condo-in-litigation/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">buying a condo in litigation</a> explains what one means for a buyer.</span></li><li><span class="i" aria-hidden="true"><svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="22" height="22" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M5.5 7h8A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 15 8.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5h-8A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 4 19.5v-11A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 5.5 7zM8 7V4.5A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 9.5 3h9A1.5 1.5 0 0 1 20 4.5v11a1.5 1.5 0 0 1-1.5 1.5H15M9.5 17.5l-2.3-2.4a1.4 1.4 0 0 1 2.3-1.6 1.4 1.4 0 0 1 2.3 1.6z"/></svg></span><span><strong>The seller's balance with the HOA.</strong> We get the association's statement of what the seller owes before closing.</span></li></ol><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Under South Carolina's condo law, unpaid assessments are a lien on the unit that ranks ahead of every other lien except property tax liens and recorded mortgages and other recorded liens.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">A buyer, other than one at a foreclosure sale, is jointly liable with the seller for what the seller owes up to the sale. The buyer's share is limited to the amount on the statement the association must issue when the buyer asks.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Where are the bargains?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">With an owner in trouble in a sound building. In our own files, condos bought from a distressed owner in a stable building have averaged a 22.32 percent discount. The building stays warrantable; only that one unit was mismanaged.</p><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Our <a href="/invest/condo-buildings/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">oceanfront building directory</a> lists buildings and their fees, and the <a href="/submarkets/myrtle-beach/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">Myrtle Beach guide</a> names the city's busiest oceanfront buildings. When it is time to sell, read <a href="/sell/sell-my-condo/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline">selling a condo in Myrtle Beach</a>.</p></div></section><section style="background:var(--ivory)" id="myrtle-beach-condo-investing-faq"><div class="wrap"><p style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:.7rem;letter-spacing:.14em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--brass);margin-bottom:.5rem;font-weight:600">Common questions</p><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.7rem;color:var(--navy);margin-bottom:1rem;line-height:1.3">Myrtle Beach condo investing FAQ</h2><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are Myrtle Beach condo prices dropping?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes, slowly. The typical metro condo is worth about 11.5 percent less than at its October 2023 high, and about 1 percent less than a year ago. House values held.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Why are HOA fees so high in Myrtle Beach?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Oceanfront buildings charge the most: the middle oceanfront condo for sale charges about $925 a month, against about $650 for all condos. The fee usually includes the building's insurance.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Are Myrtle Beach condos in a flood zone?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">About 1 in 6 condos sold in Horry County in the past year sat in a high-risk flood zone. About 1 in 10 other homes did. We read the building's flood zone and master policy for you.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Do you need to live in a Myrtle Beach condo to get the lower tax rate?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">Yes. The 4 percent rate is for the home its owner lives in, once the owner applies for it. A rental or a second home is taxed on 6 percent of its value.</p><h3 style="font-family:var(--serif);font-size:1.25rem;color:var(--navy);margin:1.6rem 0 .6rem">Is North Myrtle Beach or Myrtle Beach cheaper for a condo?</h3><p style="color:var(--muted);line-height:1.75;max-width:720px;margin-bottom:1rem">The City of Myrtle Beach. Condos there sold for a middle price of about $175,000, against about $340,000 in North Myrtle Beach.</p><p style="font-size:.78rem;color:var(--muted);line-height:1.7;max-width:760px;margin-top:1.6rem"><strong style="color:var(--navy)">Sources:</strong> <a href="https://www.zillow.com/research/data/" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Zillow Research</a>, <a href="https://acclaimweb.horrycounty.org/AcclaimWeb/search/SearchTypeDocType" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County deed index</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycounty.org/parcelapp/rest/services/HorryCountyGISApp/MapServer/24" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County parcel and flood map layers</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t12c043.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 12-43</a>, <a href="https://www.scstatehouse.gov/code/t27c031.php" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SC Code 27-31</a>, <a href="https://www.horrycountysc.gov/media/kufln4qp/tax-levy-2026_2.pdf" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Horry County 2026 tax levy</a>, <a href="https://selling-guide.fanniemae.com/sel/b4-2.1-03/ineligible-projects" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Fannie Mae B4-2.1-03</a>, <a href="https://entp.hud.gov/idapp/html/condlook.cfm" style="color:var(--navy);text-decoration:underline" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">HUD condo list</a>. Sales: county deeds, 12 months to September 2026. Homes for sale: CCMLS listings on our site, counted in total. Flood: FEMA zones at each sale's parcel point. Not legal, tax or loan advice.</p><div class="c3ph-credits"><div class="c3ph-h" id="photo-credits">Photo credits</div><div role="list" aria-labelledby="photo-credits"><div role="listitem">Two tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front. Photo: U.S. Geological Survey, <a href="https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Public domain</a>, via <a href="https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">U.S. Geological Survey</a>. Chapter3 Realty cropped and resized the photo.</div><div role="listitem">Chapter3 Realty resized these photos and cropped some of them.</div></div></div><script type="application/ld+json">{"@context":"https://schema.org","@graph":[{"@type":"ImageObject","contentUrl":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp","url":"https://chapter3realty.com/images/2026-11-a/invest-condos/myrtle-beach-condo-towers-2011-1200w.webp","width":1200,"height":675,"encodingFormat":"image/webp","name":"Two tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front","caption":"Condo towers and smaller condo buildings on the ocean in Myrtle Beach, seen from a plane in 2011. Almost half of the county's 41,000 condo units are in complexes of 100 units or more.","description":"Two tall oceanfront condo towers behind the dunes in Myrtle Beach, with smaller condo buildings across a curving road to the right and the beach and ocean in front","creator":{"@type":"Organization","name":"U.S. Geological Survey"},"creditText":"U.S","copyrightNotice":"Public domain (U.S. Geological Survey)","license":"https://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","acquireLicensePage":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","isBasedOn":"https://doi.org/10.5066/F7N877XX","contentLocation":{"@type":"Place","name":"Oceanfront in the City of Myrtle Beach, north of the city center (tax district 880), Myrtle Beach, South Carolina"},"representativeOfPage":true}]}</script></div></section><section style="background:var(--navy);padding:3rem 0"><div class="wrap" style="text-align:center;max-width:720px;margin:0 auto"><h2 style="font-family:var(--serif)